This is Schinzel's Hypothesis H, a well-known open conjecture in number theory that generalizes the Bunyakovsky conjecture and the twin prime conjecture. It is listed in the Formal Conjectures library under `@[category research open]`, indicating it is a recognized open research problem.

The conjecture states that for a finite set of integer polynomials satisfying both the Bunyakovsky condition (each polynomial is irreducible, has positive leading coefficient, and the coefficients are collectively coprime) and the Schinzel condition (no prime divides the product of all $f_i(n)$ for every integer $n$), there are infinitely many natural numbers $n$ such that every $f_i(n)$ is prime.

This problem remains unsolved. Even the simplest nontrivial special case — the twin prime conjecture (taking $f_1(n)=n$, $f_2(n)=n+2$) — is open, though Zhang, Maynard, and Tao's bounded-gap breakthroughs have made partial progress. No proof of the full Hypothesis H is known, and I cannot produce one.

